data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc`'d `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** Both work without issues:

```csharp
Span<nint>  span  = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> uspan = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit system each element is 8 bytes (`nint.Size == 8`). No special properties or unsafe context are needed beyond `AllowUnsafeBlocks` — though in modern C# even that isn't required when assigning `stackalloc` directly to a `Span<T>` (it compiled fine here either way). Native-sized integers (`nint`/`nuint`) are valid unmanaged types, so they satisfy the `stackalloc` constraint.
